"""Seed bank adapted from the approved 25-question DBA & Data Governance assessment.

Format: topic | difficulty | question | A | B | C | D | key | assessor explanation.
No answer key is exposed through the candidate API.
"""
ROWS = """Backup & recovery|Intermediate|A production SQL Server database uses the FULL recovery model. A bad update occurs at 10:07. Valid backups are available and a transaction-log backup covers 10:06. Which method can recover the database to 10:06?|Restore the latest full backup only.|Restore a suitable full backup and required log backups, using STOPAT 10:06.|Restore only the most recent differential backup.|Switch the database to SIMPLE and restart SQL Server.|B|A point-in-time restore under FULL recovery uses a suitable data backup and the required sequence of log backups, stopping at the chosen time.
Recovery models|Advanced|A DBA changes a database from FULL to SIMPLE recovery, then switches it back to FULL. What must be done to resume a normal transaction-log backup chain?|Continue the previous log-backup chain without any new data backup.|Create a new full or differential database backup before resuming log backups.|Run DBCC CHECKDB to reconnect the log chain.|Take a copy-only log backup to reconnect the previous chain.|B|Switching to SIMPLE interrupts the log chain; a new full or differential backup establishes a new base.
Restore sequence|Intermediate|A full backup was taken Sunday, a differential backup based on it Wednesday at 02:00, and transaction-log backups continued every 30 minutes. To restore to Wednesday 04:17, which sequence is appropriate?|Sunday full, Wednesday differential, then required log backups with STOPAT 04:17.|Wednesday differential alone, with RECOVERY.|Sunday full followed only by the latest available log backup.|The log backup at 04:30 alone, with STOPAT 04:17.|A|A differential depends on its base full backup; the required log sequence then advances recovery to the target time.
High availability|Advanced|For an Always On Availability Group to perform automatic failover after a primary outage, which condition set is required?|An asynchronous secondary and manual failover on both replicas.|A synchronized secondary, but no Windows cluster quorum.|Synchronous-commit replicas configured for automatic failover, a synchronized target, and cluster quorum.|Any readable secondary, even if it is not synchronized.|C|Automatic failover requires synchronous-commit automatic-failover partners, a synchronized target, and healthy WSFC quorum.
Integrity checks|Foundation|An administrator suspects database-page corruption following storage errors. Which SQL Server command is most appropriate to check database consistency?|DBCC SHRINKDATABASE|DBCC CHECKDB|UPDATE STATISTICS|CHECKPOINT|B|DBCC CHECKDB performs consistency checks across the database, including allocation and structural checks.
Blocking diagnostics|Intermediate|A production application has many queries waiting behind another session. What is the best first diagnostic step?|Restart the SQL Server service immediately.|Inspect blocking_session_id, active requests, transaction state, and relevant SQL text.|Disable all nonclustered indexes on the database.|Set every session to READ UNCOMMITTED.|B|Blocking should first be identified through live request/session and transaction information before a corrective action.
Query performance|Intermediate|After a deployment, a previously fast query becomes much slower. The DBA suspects an execution-plan regression. Which SQL Server capability best supports comparing plan and runtime history?|SQL Server Agent job history|Database Mail|Query Store|SQL Server error log|C|Query Store retains query plans and runtime statistics for investigating changes and plan regressions.
Indexing & T-SQL|Intermediate|Assume OrderDate is an indexed datetime2 column. Which filter is generally most index-friendly for selecting all orders on 1 November 2026?|WHERE CONVERT(date, OrderDate) = '2026-11-01'|WHERE YEAR(OrderDate) = 2026 AND MONTH(OrderDate) = 11 AND DAY(OrderDate) = 1|WHERE OrderDate >= '20261101' AND OrderDate < '20261102'|WHERE FORMAT(OrderDate, 'yyyy-MM-dd') = '2026-11-01'|C|A half-open date range avoids applying a function to the indexed column and is normally SARGable.
tempdb troubleshooting|Advanced|Monitoring confirms high tempdb allocation-page PAGELATCH contention under concurrent workload. Which mitigation is most directly relevant?|Set every user database to SIMPLE recovery.|Run DBCC SHRINKFILE continuously on tempdb.|Assess adding equally sized tempdb data files and verify wait-statistic improvement.|Disable SQL Server Agent.|C|Properly sized and balanced tempdb data files can reduce hot allocation-page contention.
Least privilege|Foundation|A reporting application needs read-only access to tables in one schema and no administrative capability. What is the most appropriate permission design?|Add the login to the sysadmin server role.|Add its user to db_owner.|Grant schema-level SELECT to a dedicated database role and assign the application user.|Grant CONTROL SERVER to the login.|C|A dedicated role with SELECT access to only the required schema implements least privilege.
Encryption & DR|Intermediate|A database uses Transparent Data Encryption (TDE). What is essential for restoring its encrypted backup on another SQL Server instance?|Only the source instance name.|The appropriate TDE protector certificate and private key (or corresponding key-manager access).|The SQL Server Agent operator list.|The original tempdb files.|B|The target must be able to access the protector needed to decrypt the database encryption key.
Concurrency & isolation|Advanced|Readers and writers frequently block each other under default READ COMMITTED locking. Which database option enables row-versioned READ COMMITTED reads and can reduce reader-writer blocking?|AUTO_CLOSE ON|READ_COMMITTED_SNAPSHOT ON|AUTO_SHRINK ON|TRUSTWORTHY ON|B|READ_COMMITTED_SNAPSHOT makes READ COMMITTED statements read row versions, reducing many read/write lock conflicts.
Disaster recovery targets|Foundation|A critical database has an RPO of 10 minutes and an RTO of 45 minutes. What does this mean?|At most 10 minutes of data loss and a recovery-time target of 45 minutes.|At most 45 minutes of data loss and a recovery-time target of 10 minutes.|A mandatory full backup every 10 minutes and a log backup every 45 minutes.|Zero data loss and zero downtime in every disaster.|A|RPO expresses acceptable data-loss exposure; RTO expresses the target time to restore service.
Ownership & accountability|Foundation|Who should be accountable for the approved business meaning, access policy, and quality expectations of a critical customer dataset?|Data owner|Report developer|SQL Server service account|Backup operator|A|The data owner is accountable for business decisions concerning the dataset, with stewards and technical teams implementing controls.
Governance operating model|Intermediate|Two business departments cannot agree on the ownership of a shared critical data element. What is the most effective governance response?|Allow both departments to publish conflicting definitions indefinitely.|Let the DBA choose whichever definition is easier to query.|Escalate through the defined governance decision process and document the accountable owner.|Remove the data element from the business glossary.|C|A governance council or agreed escalation process resolves cross-domain decision rights and records an accountable decision.
Data quality: completeness|Intermediate|A mandatory customer_id field contains 20,000 records: 1,500 NULL values and 500 empty strings, with no overlap. Treat both as missing. What is the completeness rate?|90%|92.5%|97.5%|10%|A|Valid values are 20,000 - 1,500 - 500 = 18,000, so completeness is 18,000 / 20,000 = 90%.
Data quality dimensions|Foundation|A customer master table is required to have exactly one row per customer_id, but many customer_id values appear more than once. Which data quality dimension is primarily violated?|Timeliness|Completeness|Uniqueness|Accuracy|C|Repeated keys violate uniqueness, even when every row has a non-null customer_id.
Data lineage|Intermediate|A finance KPI disagrees with operational source data. Which governance asset best helps trace its originating tables, transformation logic, and downstream report?|Backup retention policy|End-to-end data lineage|Server CPU dashboard|Database maintenance plan|B|Lineage identifies source-to-target movement, transformations, and dependencies.
Business glossary|Intermediate|Sales defines an active customer as active in 30 days, while Finance uses 90 days for the same executive KPI. What should happen first?|Change one dashboard query without notifying stakeholders.|Store both calculations under the same KPI name without qualification.|Delete all existing KPI history.|Have accountable stakeholders approve a standard definition and document its business rules in the glossary.|D|Approved common business definitions and documented calculation rules prevent inconsistent interpretation.
Classification & protection|Foundation|A dataset includes national ID numbers, personal phone numbers, and home addresses. What is the best governance action?|Classify it as public because the information is stored in a database.|Classify it as sensitive according to policy and apply appropriate access and protection controls.|Give all analysts unrestricted access to speed up reporting.|Remove ownership metadata to avoid exposing the department.|B|Direct identifiers and contact details warrant appropriate sensitive-data classification and safeguards.
Controlled access|Intermediate|An analyst only needs monthly aggregated customer counts but requests unrestricted access to the raw customer table with identifiers. What is the preferred response?|Grant unrestricted table access since the analyst is an employee.|Provide an approved aggregated view or dataset with only necessary access, and record authorization.|Share a sysadmin credential with the analyst.|Copy the entire customer table to a public file share.|B|Data minimization and least privilege meet the business requirement without exposing unnecessary personal data.
Retention & legal hold|Advanced|A customer dataset has reached its scheduled deletion date, but a valid legal hold applies. What should the organization do?|Delete all copies immediately because the retention date passed.|Preserve the held data under controlled access, document the exception, and delete when the hold is lifted and policy permits.|Keep the dataset forever without reassessment.|Move the dataset to an unprotected spreadsheet and delete the database copy.|B|A valid legal hold takes precedence over normal deletion and exceptions must be documented.
Master data management|Advanced|CRM and billing systems contain conflicting records for the same customer. Which practice most directly creates a trusted, reconciled customer identity?|Database index rebuilding|Transaction log shipping|Master data management with matching, survivorship, and stewardship|Ad hoc dashboard filters|C|MDM resolves cross-system entities through matching and survivorship rules with stewardship.
Change impact management|Intermediate|A source system plans to rename customer_id to client_id, and multiple ETL pipelines and dashboards may depend on the column. What is the best control before deployment?|Deploy the rename immediately, then wait for user complaints.|Rename the column and disable alerting for one week.|Ask each consumer to discover the change independently.|Use lineage and metadata to perform impact analysis, coordinate a versioned change, and notify impacted owners.|D|Metadata, lineage, and change management identify downstream risks before schema changes.
Data quality incident|Intermediate|A daily ingestion job suddenly loads many rows with NULL contract_id, a mandatory business key. Which response best follows controlled data quality management?|Silently substitute 0 for all missing contract IDs.|Disable validation rules permanently so the pipeline remains green.|Flag or quarantine affected records, notify owners/stewards, investigate the source, and track remediation.|Delete the failed job logs and continue publishing the dataset.|C|Controlled handling separates suspect records, alerts accountable parties, and documents remediation.
"""

def get_bank():
    bank = []
    for i, raw in enumerate(ROWS.strip().splitlines(), 1):
        pieces = raw.split("|")
        if len(pieces) != 9:
            raise ValueError(f"Invalid question {i}: expected 9 fields, got {len(pieces)}")
        topic, difficulty, prompt, a, b, c, d, answer, reason = pieces
        if answer not in "ABCD":
            raise ValueError(f"Invalid answer for question {i}")
        bank.append({
            "position": i, "domain": "DBA" if i <= 13 else "Data Governance",
            "topic": topic, "difficulty": difficulty, "text": prompt,
            "options": dict(zip("ABCD", (a,b,c,d))),
            "correct_code": answer, "explanation": reason,
        })
    assert len(bank) == 25
    assert sum(row["domain"] == "DBA" for row in bank) == 13
    return bank
